WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.488 --> 00:00:07.030
JUSTIN MAHOOD: Hi,
I'm Justin Mahood.

00:00:07.030 --> 00:00:09.212
I'm a product manager
on Cloud Run for Anthos.

00:00:09.212 --> 00:00:10.420
ADAM KANE: And I'm Adam Kane.

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:12.822
I'm an engineering manager
on Cloud Run for Anthos.

00:00:12.822 --> 00:00:14.280
JUSTIN MAHOOD: And
we're here today

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:17.160
to show you how to build a
scalable web application using

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:20.790
Cloud Run for Anthos on GKE.

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:22.830
So first, I'll give
you an overview

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:25.620
of Cloud Run for Anthos,
which is the technology that's

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:28.480
at the core of the sample that
we want to show you today.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:30.360
Then, we'll talk through
some of the problems

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:32.880
that you'll need to solve when
standing up an enterprise web

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:35.435
application and a
Kubernetes environment.

00:00:35.435 --> 00:00:37.560
And then we'll walk through
our sample architecture

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:40.650
and talk about the different
tools in the Anthos and GKE

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:42.420
ecosystem that
you'll use to deliver

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:43.995
this kind of application.

00:00:43.995 --> 00:00:45.870
And then, finally, I'll
give you a short demo

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:47.460
of how to deploy
our sample directly

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:50.020
into your GCP environment.

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:52.050
So let's introduce you
to the product that's

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:56.510
at the core of this sample,
Cloud Run for Anthos.

00:00:56.510 --> 00:00:58.180
When developing
web applications,

00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:00.940
serverless patterns
are very, very popular.

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:02.240
And why is that?

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:03.820
Well, there's the
operational model.

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:05.780
You don't have to worry
about infrastructure.

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:06.970
It's inherently scalable.

00:01:06.970 --> 00:01:09.340
And you pay for what you use.

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:11.710
But for developers, it's
really the programming model

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:12.670
that's attractive.

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:14.920
They also don't have to worry
about the infrastructure

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:16.000
management.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:17.500
And fundamentally,
it's optimized

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:22.020
for service-based architectures,
like web applications.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:24.210
But at the same time,
we've got Kubernetes,

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:27.300
which is now the de facto
platform for running containers

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:30.420
in the enterprise and the de
facto enterprise operations

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:34.180
platform in general.

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:36.240
So what if we can
have the benefits

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:38.250
of a serverless
development platform

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:41.520
with all of the control and
flexibility of Kubernetes?

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:46.000
And that's exactly why we
created Cloud Run for Anthos.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:47.750
You can also think of
Cloud Run for Anthos

00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:50.000
as Kubernetes the easy way.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:51.832
Developers get that
serverless experience

00:01:51.832 --> 00:01:53.540
where they can focus
on their application

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:55.068
and their application
logic, rather

00:01:55.068 --> 00:01:57.110
than worrying about the
intricacies of Kubernetes

00:01:57.110 --> 00:01:59.118
deployments and all
of the Kubernetes

00:01:59.118 --> 00:02:00.410
concepts that go along with it.

00:02:03.323 --> 00:02:04.990
So you can say that
Cloud Run for Anthos

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:06.407
gives you the best
of both worlds.

00:02:06.407 --> 00:02:08.380
It gives you the
best of serverless.

00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:11.190
You can run any standard
Docker container.

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:12.850
It has request-based
auto scaling.

00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:15.130
It's very fast and
scales down to zero.

00:02:15.130 --> 00:02:17.450
It includes incremental
rollouts and rollback

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:19.450
so you can do things like
blue-green deployments

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:21.980
and roll back for production.

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:24.600
And it also has a whole suite
of monitoring and logging tools

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:27.660
that are built in for the
developer out of the box.

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:29.970
And at the same time,
the operations team

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:32.520
gets all the benefits of
Kubernetes environment.

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:34.170
The flexibility and
control that comes

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:37.800
with GKE, integrated
policy support, the ability

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:41.850
to run your compute completely
within a VPC, custom note types

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:44.270
and customization of
the underlying hardware,

00:02:44.270 --> 00:02:46.715
and the extensive toolset
that the Kubernetes ecosystem

00:02:46.715 --> 00:02:47.215
provides.

00:02:51.118 --> 00:02:52.660
And I'd be remiss
if I didn't mention

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:55.120
that Cloud Run for Anthos
is based on the Knative Open

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:56.212
Source Project.

00:02:56.212 --> 00:02:57.670
This is an open
source project that

00:02:57.670 --> 00:03:00.220
was started by Google with
contributions from over 80

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:01.180
companies.

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:02.745
And really, what
this guarantees is

00:03:02.745 --> 00:03:04.120
that the workloads
that you write

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:06.250
and architect for
Cloud Run for Anthos

00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:08.260
are portable between
Knative environments

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:13.020
and whichever cloud platform
you ultimately end up choosing.

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:16.590
So now let's talk about actually
building and architecting

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:18.790
a web application.

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:21.480
So let's imagine for a moment.

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:25.980
You're a developer in
an e-commerce company.

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:28.600
GKE and Kubernetes
is the ops platform

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:31.530
that your company has
chosen and standardized on.

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:34.410
And you're given a task with
delivering a line of business

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:37.080
web application for tracking
your warehouse inventory

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:38.950
on a pretty tight timeline.

00:03:38.950 --> 00:03:39.930
So it's Monday morning.

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:41.472
You sit down with
your cup of coffee.

00:03:41.472 --> 00:03:42.897
You sharpen your pencil.

00:03:42.897 --> 00:03:44.730
And you start sketching
out the architecture

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:46.815
that you're going to use.

00:03:46.815 --> 00:03:49.065
And this is sort of what's
in your head to begin with.

00:03:49.065 --> 00:03:50.765
You know that it's
going to run in GCP.

00:03:50.765 --> 00:03:52.140
You know that
there's going to be

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:54.060
a web application of some kind.

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:56.190
And you know that
it ultimately needs

00:03:56.190 --> 00:04:01.300
to run on both desktop,
laptop, and mobile devices.

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:03.420
But then you really
start to break it down.

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:05.130
You know you're going
to need a frontend.

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:07.740
You're know you're going to
need a backend and a database

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:08.660
to store your state.

00:04:11.858 --> 00:04:13.400
You start breaking
it down some more.

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:16.459
And some more requirements
really start to come into view.

00:04:16.459 --> 00:04:18.513
Since your company is
standardized on GKE,

00:04:18.513 --> 00:04:20.180
you're going to use
Cloud Run for Anthos

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:21.890
for your backend
services for all

00:04:21.890 --> 00:04:24.182
of those serverless benefits
in the Kubernetes platform

00:04:24.182 --> 00:04:25.760
we talked about earlier.

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:28.730
And also, you know you're going
to need to access and modify

00:04:28.730 --> 00:04:31.080
data in a database
in a secure fashion.

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:33.508
So in addition to using
Firestore as a database,

00:04:33.508 --> 00:04:36.050
you want to be able to use your
Kubernetes service identities

00:04:36.050 --> 00:04:38.862
for this authentication.

00:04:38.862 --> 00:04:41.070
And it'd be remiss if you
hadn't thought a little bit

00:04:41.070 --> 00:04:44.520
about how load balancing and
your URLs were going to work.

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:46.890
And you realize because you
want to serve your frontend

00:04:46.890 --> 00:04:48.347
from a [INAUDIBLE]
storage bucket,

00:04:48.347 --> 00:04:49.930
but have your backend
services running

00:04:49.930 --> 00:04:51.960
in Cloud Run for
Anthos, you need the app

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:54.720
to be on a single
endpoint and subdomain

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:55.832
for [INAUDIBLE] reasons.

00:04:55.832 --> 00:04:58.290
And you need path-based routing
between the frontend served

00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:00.510
from GCS and your
multiple backend services

00:05:00.510 --> 00:05:02.550
running on Cloud Run for Anthos.

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:05.010
And also, your enterprise
has a hard core requirement

00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:07.230
that everything needs to
be HTTPS out of the box,

00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:09.960
as it should be, and that
your GKE cluster can't

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:13.060
be connected to the internet.

00:05:13.060 --> 00:05:15.090
And finally, as the
last requirements

00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:17.100
start to come into
view, you realize

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:19.860
that you need to make sure this
web application is accessible

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:20.940
only by employees.

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:22.680
They absolutely have
to authenticate.

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:24.930
And they need to use your
company's federated identity

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:26.420
provider.

00:05:26.420 --> 00:05:29.250
Also, there's different
role-based features

00:05:29.250 --> 00:05:31.513
for workers and managers
of the warehouse.

00:05:31.513 --> 00:05:33.930
So you need to have role-based
access and an authorization

00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:36.080
platform for each call
that goes through.

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:39.120
And you're going to
use Istio for that.

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:41.630
So after a couple of weeks
of hard work of architecting

00:05:41.630 --> 00:05:43.670
and writing the
application, you're

00:05:43.670 --> 00:05:46.370
ready to hand it off and
start managing inventory

00:05:46.370 --> 00:05:48.950
in your warehouse, using
an application you designed

00:05:48.950 --> 00:05:51.500
to run on Cloud Run for Anthos.

00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:53.472
But you sort of
thought to yourself,

00:05:53.472 --> 00:05:54.930
it sure would be
great if there was

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:56.190
a blueprint for all of this.

00:05:58.810 --> 00:06:00.800
And it turns out that there is--

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:03.552
the Cloud Run for Anthos
Reference web application.

00:06:03.552 --> 00:06:04.260
You see the link.

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:06.420
It's hosted up there
on GitHub today.

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:08.400
And it's an opinionated
set of best practices

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:11.040
aimed at demonstrating
a reference architecture

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:14.760
for building an enterprise
grade web application on GCP,

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:17.003
using Cloud Run for Anthos.

00:06:17.003 --> 00:06:19.170
So now I'm going to kick
it over to my friend, Adam.

00:06:19.170 --> 00:06:20.795
And he's going to
take you through each

00:06:20.795 --> 00:06:22.620
of the components
of the architecture

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:24.360
that we talked about
a little bit earlier

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:26.527
and go into a deep dive
about how we implement those

00:06:26.527 --> 00:06:28.740
and how you can implement
them in your environment.

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:30.682
Adam?

00:06:30.682 --> 00:06:31.890
ADAM KANE: Thank you, Justin.

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:34.110
As Justin mentioned,
I'm going to walk you

00:06:34.110 --> 00:06:37.710
through a little bit of a deeper
look at some of the decisions

00:06:37.710 --> 00:06:42.710
that we've made in implementing
our reference web application.

00:06:42.710 --> 00:06:45.880
So we'll start with a quick
overview of the reference

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:48.610
architecture that we've used.

00:06:48.610 --> 00:06:50.170
The goal of our
reference application

00:06:50.170 --> 00:06:53.440
is to simulate a line of
business web application that's

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:56.530
built around the Cloud
Run for Anthos platform.

00:06:56.530 --> 00:06:59.020
The subject matter of our
application in this case

00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:00.860
is inventory management.

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:02.830
But this is mostly arbitrary.

00:07:02.830 --> 00:07:05.710
The goal of this application
is to provide a reference

00:07:05.710 --> 00:07:07.660
for implementing
common web application

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:09.970
patterns using
Cloud Run for Anthos

00:07:09.970 --> 00:07:13.500
in combination with
other GCP services.

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:15.780
In this case, we've
implemented the application

00:07:15.780 --> 00:07:17.700
using Angular and Go.

00:07:17.700 --> 00:07:20.580
But the infrastructure patterns
that we demonstrate here

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:23.570
are not specific
to these languages.

00:07:23.570 --> 00:07:27.120
So as a quick overview
of the architecture,

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:30.830
it's comprised of a frontend UI
and multiple backend stateless

00:07:30.830 --> 00:07:32.090
services.

00:07:32.090 --> 00:07:34.070
It's exposed through
a Google Cloud load

00:07:34.070 --> 00:07:36.560
balancer, which provides
a consolidated endpoint

00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:38.240
to our end user.

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:41.990
The frontend UI is statically
served from a GCS bucket.

00:07:41.990 --> 00:07:43.700
And our backend
services are hosted

00:07:43.700 --> 00:07:46.110
using Cloud Run for Anthos.

00:07:46.110 --> 00:07:48.390
End user authentication
and authorization

00:07:48.390 --> 00:07:52.110
of our backend API requests are
handled by Istio in combination

00:07:52.110 --> 00:07:54.480
with the Google
Identity Platform.

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:56.460
And our application
state is stored

00:07:56.460 --> 00:07:58.870
in the Firestore database.

00:07:58.870 --> 00:08:01.550
Let's now take a look in
a little bit more detail

00:08:01.550 --> 00:08:04.220
about each of the three
major infrastructure patterns

00:08:04.220 --> 00:08:08.130
that we've used in our
reference application.

00:08:08.130 --> 00:08:09.740
First, we'll talk
about the database

00:08:09.740 --> 00:08:12.950
that stores state
for our application.

00:08:12.950 --> 00:08:15.110
Of course, our
backend services need

00:08:15.110 --> 00:08:19.010
to be able to access and modify
the data in this database

00:08:19.010 --> 00:08:20.690
in a secure fashion.

00:08:20.690 --> 00:08:23.780
Ideally, we should be able
to access this database using

00:08:23.780 --> 00:08:26.870
the service identities
for our backend services

00:08:26.870 --> 00:08:31.220
and not have to manage explicit
secrets or other authentication

00:08:31.220 --> 00:08:32.929
tokens.

00:08:32.929 --> 00:08:37.309
We use Firestore in this case,
which is compatible with GCP IM

00:08:37.309 --> 00:08:38.539
authentication.

00:08:38.539 --> 00:08:41.090
But this discussion is really
relevant to any database

00:08:41.090 --> 00:08:45.340
or service, which provides
authentication using IAM.

00:08:45.340 --> 00:08:47.350
Our backend services,
because they're

00:08:47.350 --> 00:08:50.800
running on Cloud Run for Anthos
are bound to Kubernetes Service

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:52.870
Accounts, or KSAs.

00:08:52.870 --> 00:08:56.470
We use a feature of GKE called
Workload Identities to bind

00:08:56.470 --> 00:08:59.350
these Kubernetes Service
Accounts to Google Service

00:08:59.350 --> 00:09:03.280
Accounts, or GSAs, in IAM.

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:05.050
Once this binding
is in place, we

00:09:05.050 --> 00:09:07.690
can apply our access
permissions for our database

00:09:07.690 --> 00:09:09.760
to the Google Service accounts.

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:12.190
And through workload
identity, these permissions

00:09:12.190 --> 00:09:17.190
apply transitively to our
Kubernetes Service Accounts.

00:09:17.190 --> 00:09:19.200
Once we've enabled
workload identities

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:22.260
on our Cloud Run
for Anthos cluster,

00:09:22.260 --> 00:09:24.840
binding service accounts
is a two-step process

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:29.290
that requires action
in both GKE and IAM.

00:09:29.290 --> 00:09:31.890
In GKE, each Kubernetes
service account

00:09:31.890 --> 00:09:35.940
is annotated with the GCP
service account annotation,

00:09:35.940 --> 00:09:37.830
which specifies the
Google Service account

00:09:37.830 --> 00:09:40.590
that we are binding the
Kubernetes service account to.

00:09:43.390 --> 00:09:47.640
In IAM, a policy binding is
created against the Google

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:48.830
Service Account.

00:09:48.830 --> 00:09:51.170
And this policy binding
specifies the Kubernetes

00:09:51.170 --> 00:09:52.545
Service Account
and the namespace

00:09:52.545 --> 00:09:56.017
that that Google service
account will be bound to.

00:09:56.017 --> 00:09:57.600
If you're paying
close attention here,

00:09:57.600 --> 00:10:03.270
you'll notice that we don't
specify a specific GKE cluster

00:10:03.270 --> 00:10:05.230
with this policy binding.

00:10:05.230 --> 00:10:07.050
This is because workload
identity bindings

00:10:07.050 --> 00:10:09.960
apply to all Kubernetes
Service Accounts, which

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:13.140
reside in a matching
namespace across all clusters

00:10:13.140 --> 00:10:14.850
in the target project.

00:10:14.850 --> 00:10:16.560
This is an important
detail to consider

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:19.018
when you're thinking about how
to manage your GCP resources

00:10:19.018 --> 00:10:20.340
in your project structure.

00:10:20.340 --> 00:10:23.160
But keep in mind that the
binding will only take effect

00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:26.520
if the Kubernetes Service
Account is also annotated

00:10:26.520 --> 00:10:27.360
as described here.

00:10:30.750 --> 00:10:32.780
Next, we're going
to look a little bit

00:10:32.780 --> 00:10:37.490
into how we have solved load
balancing and request routing.

00:10:37.490 --> 00:10:41.190
So just a quick reminder about
our requirements, in this case,

00:10:41.190 --> 00:10:44.600
we want to have a single
endpoint and a single subdomain

00:10:44.600 --> 00:10:46.280
for our application.

00:10:46.280 --> 00:10:48.620
We need path-based
routing to route requests

00:10:48.620 --> 00:10:51.650
between our frontend
and our backend service.

00:10:51.650 --> 00:10:55.870
Our enduser traffic should
be secured with HTTPS.

00:10:55.870 --> 00:10:59.780
And to avoid exposing
our cluster ingress

00:10:59.780 --> 00:11:03.230
to the internet, we would
like to restrict access

00:11:03.230 --> 00:11:05.600
to cluster ingress if possible.

00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:08.990
It would also be very nice if we
could use Kubernetes resources

00:11:08.990 --> 00:11:11.810
to manage all of our load
balancing infrastructure

00:11:11.810 --> 00:11:14.150
for the application.

00:11:14.150 --> 00:11:15.988
Let's see in a little
bit more detail

00:11:15.988 --> 00:11:18.280
how we've addressed these
requirements in our reference

00:11:18.280 --> 00:11:20.500
web app.

00:11:20.500 --> 00:11:23.050
In this diagram, you can
see the resource layout

00:11:23.050 --> 00:11:27.315
for the load balancer used in
our reference web application.

00:11:27.315 --> 00:11:28.690
If you're not
familiar with GCLB,

00:11:28.690 --> 00:11:32.090
this diagram probably looks
a little bit intimidating.

00:11:32.090 --> 00:11:34.133
But we'll break it down
over the next few slides

00:11:34.133 --> 00:11:35.800
and look at each
section in more detail.

00:11:38.460 --> 00:11:39.870
At the heart of
our load balancer

00:11:39.870 --> 00:11:42.220
is what's known as a URL map.

00:11:42.220 --> 00:11:44.730
And our URL map
defines an association

00:11:44.730 --> 00:11:48.480
between request paths
and backend resources.

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:51.120
In this case, our URL map
includes a default route

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:54.150
to a backend bucket
associated with the GCS

00:11:54.150 --> 00:11:58.010
bucket that contains our
static application assets.

00:11:58.010 --> 00:12:00.260
It also includes
past specific routes

00:12:00.260 --> 00:12:02.630
to multiple backend
services, which

00:12:02.630 --> 00:12:04.520
are each mapped to
individual Cloud Run

00:12:04.520 --> 00:12:07.880
for Anthos services
running on our cluster.

00:12:07.880 --> 00:12:10.910
By default, a custom
host header is

00:12:10.910 --> 00:12:14.900
required to ensure that requests
received by the cluster ingress

00:12:14.900 --> 00:12:17.870
are sent along to the correct
service on our cluster.

00:12:17.870 --> 00:12:21.380
In this case, we're using GCLB
to inject these host header

00:12:21.380 --> 00:12:23.532
values and to make sure
that our requests are

00:12:23.532 --> 00:12:25.490
sent to the appropriate
service on the cluster.

00:12:28.660 --> 00:12:30.940
In the case of the
reference application,

00:12:30.940 --> 00:12:34.210
we've configured our load
balancer to terminate SSL.

00:12:34.210 --> 00:12:38.070
We've used a Google-managed
SSL certificate for this case.

00:12:41.220 --> 00:12:42.990
Because our end
user traffic always

00:12:42.990 --> 00:12:46.500
starts at the GCLB endpoint,
our cluster ingress

00:12:46.500 --> 00:12:50.010
doesn't need to be exposed
directly to the internet.

00:12:50.010 --> 00:12:53.940
Traffic to the
ingress of the cluster

00:12:53.940 --> 00:12:56.700
can be restricted
to only be allowed

00:12:56.700 --> 00:13:00.280
from trusted
sources such as GCLB

00:13:00.280 --> 00:13:02.640
as well known ingress ranges.

00:13:02.640 --> 00:13:05.670
This can be achieved through
either using a private cluster,

00:13:05.670 --> 00:13:07.770
or, as in the case with
our reference application,

00:13:07.770 --> 00:13:10.380
using firewalls to restrict
where that traffic can

00:13:10.380 --> 00:13:11.940
originate from.

00:13:11.940 --> 00:13:14.190
When you provision our
reference web application

00:13:14.190 --> 00:13:17.310
with the automation
we've included in GitHub,

00:13:17.310 --> 00:13:19.350
it will actually
automatically apply

00:13:19.350 --> 00:13:24.330
these ingress traffic rules to
restrict access to the cluster.

00:13:28.160 --> 00:13:31.520
There's a lot of individual
resources to provision

00:13:31.520 --> 00:13:34.950
and manage with a load
balancer like this.

00:13:34.950 --> 00:13:38.280
The config connector, or
KCC, provides the ability

00:13:38.280 --> 00:13:41.670
to manage all of these
resources using KRM.

00:13:41.670 --> 00:13:44.730
And they do this by defining
Kubernetes CRDs for each

00:13:44.730 --> 00:13:47.190
of these GCP resources.

00:13:47.190 --> 00:13:49.140
In the case of our
reference application,

00:13:49.140 --> 00:13:52.200
we install KCC on
the same cluster used

00:13:52.200 --> 00:13:54.720
to host our backend services.

00:13:54.720 --> 00:13:56.370
Infrastructure for
our application

00:13:56.370 --> 00:13:58.410
can then be
provisioned and managed

00:13:58.410 --> 00:14:02.720
by creating KCC resources
on the same cluster.

00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:05.390
You can reference and reuse
any of the infrastructure

00:14:05.390 --> 00:14:08.000
configuration that we talk
about in this presentation

00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:11.091
by referencing the KRM
in our GitHub repository.

00:14:14.250 --> 00:14:16.410
You can connect
Cloud Run for Anthos

00:14:16.410 --> 00:14:20.940
to a GCLB load balancer using
a standalone Network Endpoint

00:14:20.940 --> 00:14:23.890
Group, or an NEG.

00:14:23.890 --> 00:14:28.300
Cloud Run for Anthos uses Istio
to manage its request ingress

00:14:28.300 --> 00:14:29.830
to the cluster.

00:14:29.830 --> 00:14:32.740
Even when you don't
explicitly install Istio,

00:14:32.740 --> 00:14:34.270
we package a minimal
version of it

00:14:34.270 --> 00:14:37.606
with Cloud Run for Anthos when
you install it on the cluster.

00:14:37.606 --> 00:14:40.880
All requests that are
bound for user services

00:14:40.880 --> 00:14:42.470
on your Cloud Run
for Anthos cluster

00:14:42.470 --> 00:14:45.830
first arrive at what's called
the Istio Ingress Gateway

00:14:45.830 --> 00:14:48.230
Service on the
cluster and are then

00:14:48.230 --> 00:14:52.200
routed from there to the
appropriate user service.

00:14:52.200 --> 00:14:55.980
To connect to GCLB, we are going
to create a Network Endpoint

00:14:55.980 --> 00:15:00.210
Group associated with the
ingress gateway on our cluster.

00:15:00.210 --> 00:15:05.850
To do this, we add a
cloud.google.com/neg annotation

00:15:05.850 --> 00:15:08.520
to the Istio ingress
gateway service resource.

00:15:08.520 --> 00:15:12.880
And this is just a standard
Kubernetes service resource.

00:15:12.880 --> 00:15:17.140
In response, GKE will add
the NEG status annotation

00:15:17.140 --> 00:15:19.570
automatically to that
service resource.

00:15:19.570 --> 00:15:22.180
And this annotation
will include the ID

00:15:22.180 --> 00:15:25.080
and the zone of the associated
network endpoint group.

00:15:28.520 --> 00:15:31.940
We'll now create a backend
service to associate

00:15:31.940 --> 00:15:34.800
with this Network
Endpoint Group.

00:15:34.800 --> 00:15:40.080
We want to bind our GCLB backend
service to a specific end user

00:15:40.080 --> 00:15:42.670
service on the cluster.

00:15:42.670 --> 00:15:45.360
So we configure
the backend service

00:15:45.360 --> 00:15:49.440
with a custom request
header for the host header

00:15:49.440 --> 00:15:52.660
field of the request.

00:15:52.660 --> 00:15:55.170
Cloud Run for Anthos uses
this host header value

00:15:55.170 --> 00:15:58.290
to determine where to route
the requests once they

00:15:58.290 --> 00:16:01.950
arrive at the Istio
ingress gateway service.

00:16:01.950 --> 00:16:03.880
All of the requests,
as a result, forwarded

00:16:03.880 --> 00:16:08.460
to this backend service will
arrive at our desired user

00:16:08.460 --> 00:16:09.510
service on the cluster.

00:16:13.690 --> 00:16:16.780
The Network Endpoint Group
is then added as a backend

00:16:16.780 --> 00:16:19.570
to this backend service
using its ID end

00:16:19.570 --> 00:16:24.330
zone that we received
from the GKE annotation.

00:16:24.330 --> 00:16:25.980
In this case, the
backend service

00:16:25.980 --> 00:16:29.190
is associated with
food.example.com.

00:16:29.190 --> 00:16:31.290
And we can now include
this backend service

00:16:31.290 --> 00:16:33.820
in the URL map of
our load balancer,

00:16:33.820 --> 00:16:36.505
so that we can route
requests to it.

00:16:36.505 --> 00:16:37.880
In the examples
on this slide, we

00:16:37.880 --> 00:16:41.880
showed G Cloud commands to
provision these resources.

00:16:41.880 --> 00:16:43.730
But it's also
possible to provision

00:16:43.730 --> 00:16:46.160
these resources using
config connector.

00:16:46.160 --> 00:16:48.140
You can see an
example of this if you

00:16:48.140 --> 00:16:51.761
look at the care and resources
in our GitHub repository.

00:16:54.590 --> 00:16:56.120
Finally, we're
going to cover how

00:16:56.120 --> 00:16:58.400
we handle end user
authentication

00:16:58.400 --> 00:17:02.310
and authorization for the
reference application.

00:17:02.310 --> 00:17:05.250
As a refresher, we're
building an application here

00:17:05.250 --> 00:17:07.859
for internal use by employees.

00:17:07.859 --> 00:17:10.980
So it's important that we
authenticate each end user that

00:17:10.980 --> 00:17:13.569
arrives at our application.

00:17:13.569 --> 00:17:17.150
We would like to use a
federated identity provider.

00:17:17.150 --> 00:17:19.829
And we'd also like to
implement email and password

00:17:19.829 --> 00:17:22.349
authentication as well.

00:17:22.349 --> 00:17:25.319
We're going to need
role-based authorization

00:17:25.319 --> 00:17:27.839
to allow us to limit
access to certain features

00:17:27.839 --> 00:17:31.230
on the application,
based on a custom user

00:17:31.230 --> 00:17:33.240
role assigned to each user.

00:17:33.240 --> 00:17:35.985
In this case, users can be
either workers or managers.

00:17:39.700 --> 00:17:41.890
Let's take a look
at how we've solved

00:17:41.890 --> 00:17:45.680
authorization and authentication
on our reference application.

00:17:45.680 --> 00:17:49.060
So we've chosen to use Google
as our federated identity

00:17:49.060 --> 00:17:50.260
provider.

00:17:50.260 --> 00:17:53.140
This allows end users to
sign into the application

00:17:53.140 --> 00:17:55.040
with their Google account.

00:17:55.040 --> 00:17:58.190
We've also implemented email
and password authentication.

00:17:58.190 --> 00:18:00.130
However, this is
mostly to easily enable

00:18:00.130 --> 00:18:02.970
certain testing scenarios.

00:18:02.970 --> 00:18:06.270
Covering how this
works end-to-end,

00:18:06.270 --> 00:18:09.450
when a user authenticates
with our application,

00:18:09.450 --> 00:18:12.810
they receive a JSON Web Token.

00:18:12.810 --> 00:18:16.680
And this JSON Web Token
is used by the frontend

00:18:16.680 --> 00:18:20.640
to make API calls to
our backend services.

00:18:20.640 --> 00:18:24.450
When a request arrives at
the Istio Ingress Gateway,

00:18:24.450 --> 00:18:27.840
Istio is configured to validate
these tokens with the GCP

00:18:27.840 --> 00:18:31.020
Identity Platform
and also enforce

00:18:31.020 --> 00:18:35.375
role-based authorization
for various methods and APIs

00:18:35.375 --> 00:18:36.375
on our backend services.

00:18:40.030 --> 00:18:43.030
Here, you can see an example
of the Istio authentication

00:18:43.030 --> 00:18:45.550
policy created to
validate these JSON Web

00:18:45.550 --> 00:18:48.790
Tokens against the
Google Identity Platform.

00:18:48.790 --> 00:18:51.940
In this case, it applies to all
requests with paths that begin

00:18:51.940 --> 00:18:53.830
with the /API prefix.

00:18:57.650 --> 00:19:00.380
We also configure
authorization in this case

00:19:00.380 --> 00:19:01.820
with Istio resources.

00:19:01.820 --> 00:19:04.280
And you can see an
example of that here.

00:19:04.280 --> 00:19:07.730
In this case, this
policy authorizes access

00:19:07.730 --> 00:19:10.400
to the POST method of
the inventory transaction

00:19:10.400 --> 00:19:14.930
API for end users with
the role type worker.

00:19:14.930 --> 00:19:17.780
All of the authentication
and authorization resources

00:19:17.780 --> 00:19:19.220
used in our
reference application

00:19:19.220 --> 00:19:22.680
can be found in our
GitHub repository.

